		{"id":"7eaca157c240ac3a","x":-391,"y":-260,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"[[Cassian Draemont.canvas]]\n#PlayerCharacter "},
		{"id":"537184232e7771b0","x":-391,"y":-120,"width":2120,"height":1360,"type":"text","text":"Cassian Draemont\nRace: Human\nClass: Wizard 8 (Evocation)\nAge: Physically early 30s— chronologically over 80\nAffiliation: House Draemont of the Old Askain Empire\nOrigins of House Draemont\nHouse Draemont was one of the great noble houses that ruled the Old Askain Empire, a nation renowned for its disciplined mages, intricate bureaucracy, and mastery of refined arcana.\nThe Draemonts rose to prominence three centuries ago when High Arcanist Elora Draemont discovered that a rare crystalline substance known as Residuum could be refined into an amplifying conduit for spellcasting. Her descendants guarded its secrets jealously, using it to create wards, weapons, and wonders that rivaled divine miracles, until other parts of the world began to utilize it for their own needs. This resulted in a race of trying to obtain and refine Residuum to enhance the magical prowess of individuals who focused with the arcane. \nTheir creed was “Power, restrained through purpose.”\nBy the time Cassian was born, House Draemont’s laboratories and libraries were considered the crown of Askain’s magical progress.\nCassian’s Early Life\nCassian was the youngest son of Lord Gabriel Draemont, a cold strategist and Evoker, and Lady Marie Draemont, a bright Abjurer devoted to the study of humane magics of protection. Unlike his elder siblings who pursued court politics, Cassian was drawn to the machinery of magic itself. \nFrom a young age, he spent more time in the Draemont workshops than in the grand halls of court. He would tinker with discarded spell components, inscribe crude runes onto scraps of parchment and metal, and test the limits of how magic interacted between the weave and the physical world. Though his father found his experiments childish, his mother was the only one who truly encouraged them. To Cassian, the arcane was not merely power to be wielded, but a living system— almost something that could be understood, refined, and made better.\nBut when his mother fell in battle and passed during an early war with neighbouring nations, struck down by an unknown arcane force that ruptured her very soul in front of his own eyes, his perspective shifted. Her soft voice that once tempered his impulses was gone. His father, consumed by grief and duty, withdrew into military service, leaving Cassian to his own devices.\nThough Lord Gabriel dismissed his son’s experiments as childish indulgence, Cassian’s grief became his fuel. He devoted every waking hour to understanding the forces that had taken his mother from him, studying not only how magic functioned, but how it failed. By the time he reached adulthood, his intellect had far surpassed his peers and siblings at the Imperial Academy. He came to believe that magic was not a weapon to be honed, but a living organism to be nurtured. \nStill, Cassian was dragged to the pull of the battlefield. Pressured by his father’s expectations, he enlisted in the Arcane Vanguard. Though reluctant, he began to see it as the ultimate laboratory— a place where order and chaos collided, where spells were tested under the harshest conditions. \nThere amid blood and brilliance, he began to see how other Empires thrived on cruelty: soldiers drained to empower spells, lives sacrificed to fuel enchantments, and Residuum weaponized into atrocities.\nThat realization planted a question in his mind that later defined him:\n“If magic can create fields of beauty from chaos, why must we suffer for it? That which inspires our greatest good, is also the cause of our greatest evil.”\nFrom that point onward, Cassian Draemont sought not victory, not perfection, but balance. Harmony. The dream of a world where invention, discovery, and warfare could coexist without cruelty.\nThe War and the Fall of Askain.\nFifty years ago, the Old Askain Empire faced invasion from a necromantic order whose goal was to steal the secrets of Askanian arcane technologies.\n\nThe invaders, calling themselves the Unhileg, wielded necromancy not as corruption but as conquest— seeking to unravel the threads of time itself to bend fate in their favor. Their cruelty was deliberate, driven by an unknown purpose that even now remains enshrouded.\nAs war raged across the empire, Lord Gabriel Draemont led the defense of the capital's citadel. During the Siege of Echron Vale, the undead swarmed the city’s walls in endless waves. Gabriel was struck down by a withering curse that turned his own lifeforce against him, leaving him moments from death.\nIn desperation and grief, Cassian turned to the one theory he had never dared to test, Kinetic Sigil Integration. A ritual inscribing Residuum based runes directly onto living flesh to convert motion, magic, and life into pure energy. It was designed to harmonize the weave into a mortal— to stabilize magic through human connection.\nHe intended to use it to save his father.\nCassian brought Gabriel to the Draemont Spire and began the ritual, carving sigils into his arms, chest, and heartline. He infused his own blood with refined Residuum, attempting to bind his father’s fading essence into the weave and reawaken him. But as the final rune flared, devastation occurred. The Residuum responded not as a conduit, but as a devourer.\nThe sigils collapsed inward, absorbing Gabriel’s body into Cassian’s own as the kinetic field overloaded. Cassian felt his father’s lifeforce wither away, but all that remained was the connection from his father to the weave merge with him. He felt every ounce of power, a pure connection to the harmony of the weave throbbing in his very soul, before being consumed entirely by a kinetic surge. In the next instant, the sky burned.\nCassian emerged from the ritual changed, his veins alight with arcadic fire, the same runes inscribed upon his father now etched into his own body. He walked to the battlements and unleashed everything. Entire legions of undead disintegrated beneath a storm of fire and destruction, a wave of devastation so immense that even the living were caught in its wake.\nBut power, once unleashed, refused to stop. The Residuum in his veins began to fracture, overloading his body with energy far beyond mortal containment. Witnesses saw him kneel atop the Spire, screaming his father’s name as the symbols along his skin turned white hot, until they exploded outward in a blinding surge of red and white light that swallowed half the capital.\nWhen the smoke cleared from the Draemont Spire, the city was already gone. Only its foundations scorched into glass.\nThe Askain Empire fell soon after.\nFifty Years Later.\n(saw caesar giga chad flying across the sky towards the citadel in his prime while saving his father, a bright shining light)\n\t• Woke up in Xroas talking mid sentence to my troops in the middle of the battlemelts as Old Askain Cassian, as a cloaked figure waved their hand walked across the tent. \n\t• Fought the troops cause i said shit like who tf is giga nigga\n\t• Then they started to draw swords thinking it was mind control (WHAT)\n\t• Miscommunication is the failure of relationships rip dead bozos 2025-2025\n\t• Chased the cloaked figure outside the walls of Xroas until they threw a metal crest of Umbra V at my feet. \n\t• Runs away from Xroas to Volstar\n\t• Heads to Volsar and takes side jobs trying to make coin and understand the world he lives looking like caleb hobo guy \n\t• Sees the same bright light, thinks that's the same guy about when he died (giga chad), reason why he goes over and encounters the party\n\t• I have 50 gold worth of residuum\n"}
	],
